module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjChartKernel.Basic
public import StacksFrontier.RingTheory.GradedAlgebra.AssociatedPrimesHomogeneous

/-!
# The ideal of a closed subscheme of `Proj` is saturated

Let `S = ⨁_{n ≥ 0} 𝒮 n` be a graded ring generated in degree one over `𝒮 0`, let `X = Proj 𝒮`,
and let `i : Z ⟶ X` be a closed immersion. An ideal `I ⊆ S` is *saturated* when an element that
one whole homogeneous component `𝒮 N` multiplies into `I` already lies in `I`. This file proves
that `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpectrum.Proj.idealOfClosedSubscheme 𝒮 i` has that property.

The consequence that the `Proj` application wants is about associated primes: combined with the
graded-ring results of `StacksFrontier/RingTheory/GradedAlgebra/AssociatedPrimesHomogeneous.lean`,
every associated prime of `I(Z)` is a homogeneous prime which does **not** contain the irrelevant
ideal — that is, a point of `Proj 𝒮` rather than the irrelevant ideal itself.

The argument is the chart computation of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/ProjChartKernel/Basic.lean` read in the other direction.
Suppose `𝒮 N` multiplies a homogeneous `b ∈ 𝒮 n` into `I(Z)`, and let `f` be a degree-one
element. Taking the particular multiplier `f ^ N ∈ 𝒮 N` puts `f ^ N b` in `I(Z)`, so on the
standard open `D₊(f)` the chart function `(f ^ N b) / f ^ (N + n)` lies in `Ker(i.app D₊(f))`.
But `(f ^ N b) / f ^ (N + n)` and `b / f ^ n` are the *same* element of
`HomogeneousLocalization.Away 𝒮 f`, so the chart function of `b` itself lies in that kernel. The
defining ideal over `D₊(f)` of the zero scheme of the twisted section of `b` is generated by that
one chart function, so the zero scheme contains `Z` over `D₊(f)`; and the standard opens of the
degree-one elements cover `Proj 𝒮`, so an ideal-sheaf inequality checked there holds everywhere.
Hence the twisted section of `b` pulls back to zero on `Z`, i.e. `b ∈ I(Z)`.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpectrum.Proj.chartFraction_pow_mul_left`: multiplying the
  numerator of a chart function by `f ^ N` and the denominator by the same power does not change
  it.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpectrum.Proj`
  `.zeroScheme_degreeSection_ideal_basicOpen_of_degree_eq`: the local equation of the zero scheme
  on a standard chart, with the degree of the homogeneous element given by an equation rather
  than literally `n • d`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpectrum.Proj`
  `.mem_idealOfClosedSubscheme_of_forall_chartFraction_mem_ker`: **the chartwise criterion** —
  membership in `I(Z)` may be checked on any cover of `Proj 𝒮` by standard opens of homogeneous
  elements of one common positive degree. No hypothesis on the grading: the twists it needs
  invertible are invertible because of the cover.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpectrum.Proj.mem_idealOfClosedSubscheme_of_forall_mul_mem`: the
  homogeneous case of saturation — if a whole homogeneous component multiplies `b` into `I(Z)`
  then `b` itself lies in `I(Z)`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpectrum.Proj.saturated_idealOfClosedSubscheme`: **the ideal of a
  closed subscheme of `Proj` is saturated**, and its projective-space form
  `projectiveSpace_saturated_idealOfClosedSubscheme`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpectrum.Proj`
  `.isHomogeneous_and_not_irrelevant_le_of_mem_associatedPrimes_idealOfClosedSubscheme`: over a
  Noetherian graded ring every associated prime of `I(Z)` is homogeneous and relevant, and its
  projective-space form
  `projectiveSpace_isHomogeneous_and_not_irrelevant_le_of_mem_associatedPrimes`.

## Implementation notes

No Noetherian hypothesis is used for saturation itself. The source states the saturation formula
for a Noetherian graded ring generated in degree one, because it computes the ideal through the
torsion description of the kernel of `A ⟶ ⨁ Γ(Proj A, 𝒪(n))`, where Noetherianness is needed.
The route here is the chart comparison instead. The chartwise criterion it rests on needs no
hypothesis on the grading at all — it asks for a cover by standard opens of elements of one
common positive degree, and reads the twist-invertibility instances off that cover through
`algebraicGeometry_isInvertible_twistingSheaf_of_cover`. Degree-one generation survives in
`mem_idealOfClosedSubscheme_of_forall_mul_mem` and `saturated_idealOfClosedSubscheme` only
because the multiplier they are given lives in a single component `𝒮 N` and the chart element has
to be of degree one for `f ^ N` to be such a multiplier. Noetherianness reappears only in the
associated-primes corollary, where Mathlib's `Submodule.isAssociatedPrime_iff` consumes it.

Saturation is written out as the hypothesis `∀ x, (∃ N, ∀ y ∈ 𝒮 N, y * x ∈ I) → x ∈ I` rather
than introduced as a predicate, following
`StacksFrontier/RingTheory/GradedAlgebra/AssociatedPrimesHomogeneous.lean`: the notion already has
several spellings in this repository and coining a name here would add another.

Reduction from an arbitrary element to a homogeneous one uses that `I(Z)` is homogeneous
(`idealOfClosedSubscheme_isHomogeneous`) together with
`Stacks.GradedRing.mul_mem_isHomogeneous_iff_forall_proj`: for homogeneous `y`, the product
`y * x` lies in a homogeneous ideal exactly when all the degreewise products do. So a multiplier
that carries `x` into `I(Z)` carries each homogeneous component of `x` there as well.

Two degree casts are handled once each. `chartFraction` is indexed so that a homogeneous element
of degree `n • d` has chart function `b / f ^ n`; at `d = 1` the degree reads `n • 1` rather than
`n`, so the local equation of the zero scheme is restated with the degree given by an equation
and `subst` discharges it (`zeroScheme_degreeSection_ideal_basicOpen_of_degree_eq`, which the
chartwise criterion no longer needs, being stated at `n • d` itself, but its degree-one consumers
elsewhere still do). `SetLike.pow_mem_graded` likewise places `f ^ N` in `𝒮 (N • 1)`.

No `@[stacks 0BXK]` attribute is attached. That example's saturation assertion is an equivalence
— an ideal is of the form `I(Z)` *if and only if* it is saturated — together with the formula
`I(V₊(I)) = {x | 𝒮 n x ⊆ I for some n}`. Only the forward implication is proved here, and the
whole of that assertion is registered elsewhere, by two independent routes.

`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/ProjSaturation/VPlus.lean` carries the attribute
(`@[stacks 0BXK "standard_saturation"]` and `@[stacks 0BXK "saturated_ideals"]`), and gets there
*without* the comparison map `(Proj.map ψ)^* 𝒪_{Proj 𝒜}(n) ⟶ 𝒪_{Proj ℬ}(n)` of Tag 01N0
part (3): the ideal sheaf of `V₊(I)` on a degree-one chart is computed from the localized ideal
directly, rather than from the sections computed on `V₊(I)` by
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/ProjSaturation/Quotient.lean`.

The other route does use that comparison, which is now proved:
`AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpectrum.Proj.idealOfClosedSubscheme_map_eq_of_ker_eq_of_saturated`
of `StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/ProjSaturation/QuotientComparison.lean` is the converse for an
arbitrary surjection out of a Noetherian grading generated in degree one, through the comparison
`pullbackGlobalSections_degreeSection_eq_zero_iff_degreeSection_map_eq_zero` proved there.
At the quotient by a homogeneous ideal it is
`AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpectrum.Proj.idealOfClosedSubscheme_projMap_quotientMk_eq`.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0BXK](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BXK)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0BXL](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BXL)
